e roots are $\\lambda =\\pm \\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{\\text{i}}}={e}^{\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x), \\\\ {x}^{-\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{-\\text{i}}}={e}^{-\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{\\text{i}}+B{x}^{-\\text{i}} \\\\ y(x) & =A(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+B(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\ y(x) & =(A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite $y(x)$ as\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),\n$$\n\nwhere  ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c82de05a6ee9ebc9da262c7e07097557d386092961ee49ef5e1de5f6262d4318"} {"question" "q-64788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a1