ation\n\n$$\n2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-2t{y}^{′}+6y=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-2t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+6{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-2\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+6{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-2\\lambda +6] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-2\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ 2({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1)-1+3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2} & =-2 \\\\ \\lambda -1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =1\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{1+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}} & =t{e}^{\\text{i}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)), \\\\ {t}^{1-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}} & =t{e}^{-\\text{i}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =A{t}^{1+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}+B{t}^{1-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}} \\\\  & =At(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))+Bt(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)) \\\\  & =(A+B)t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}(A-B)t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite $y(t)$ as \n\n$$\ny(t)=t({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c82de05a6ee9ebc9da262c7e07097557d386092961ee49ef5e1de5f6262d4318"} {"question" "q-64848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(t)=t({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))"] ["math" "y(t)=t({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))"] ["math" "y(t)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2)"] ["math" "y(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t"] ["math" "y(t)={t}^{2}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(t)=t({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+12y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-5x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+12{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-5\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+12{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-5\\lambda +12] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-5\\lambda +12 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +12 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-3 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =3\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}={x}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\ {x}^{3-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}={x}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{3+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}}+B{x}^{3-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+B{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =(A+B){x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}(A-B){x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite $y(x)$ as\n\n$$\ny(x)={x}^{3}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c82de05a6ee9ebc9da262c7e07097557d386092961ee49ef5e1de5f6262d4318"} {"question" "q-64847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)=x({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))"] ["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)"] ["math" "y(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3))+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3))"] ["math" "y(x)={x}^{2}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))"] ["math" "y(x)={x}^{3}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)={x}^{3}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-93885" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+6y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-93885" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+6{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+6{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +6] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =-5 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2} & =-5 \\\\ \\lambda -1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =1\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{1+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}} & =x{e}^{\\text{i}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)), \\\\ {x}^{1-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}} & =x{e}^{-\\text{i}\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{1+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}}+B{x}^{1-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}} \\\\  & =Ax(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+Bx(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =(A+B)x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}(A-B)x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite $y(x)$ as \n\n$$\ny(x)=x({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-93885" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c82de05a6ee9ebc9da262c7e07097557d386092961ee49ef5e1de5f6262d4318"} {"question" "q-93885" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/D