orkers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+25y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-1,\\,x>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+25{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+25{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +25] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\pm 5\\text{i}.$  \n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{x}^{5\\text{i}}+B{x}^{-5\\text{i}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{5\\text{i}}}={e}^{5\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x), \\\\ {x}^{-5\\text{i}} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{-5\\text{i}}}={e}^{-5\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{5\\text{i}}+B{x}^{-5\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))+B(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$  \n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5{x}^{-1}({c}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-{c}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=-1$ and ${c}_{1}=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =5{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c82de05a6ee9ebc9da262c7e07097557d386092961ee49ef5e1de5f6262d4318"} {"question" "q-64851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)"] ["math" "y(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)"] ["math" "y(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)"] ["math" "y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)"] ["math" "y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-7t{y}^{′}+20y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2,\\,t>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-7t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+20{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-7\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+20{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-7\\lambda +20] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-7\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +20 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16)-16+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -4 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =4\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =4\\pm 2\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{4+2\\text{i}} & ={t}^{4}{e}^{\\text{i}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}={t}^{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)) \\\\ {t}^{4-2\\text{i}} & ={t}^{4}{e}^{-\\text{i}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}={t}^{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =A{t}^{4+2\\text{i}}+B{t}^{4-2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A{t}^{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))+B{t}^{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)) \\\\  & =(A+B){t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\text{i}(A-B){t}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the expression for $y(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny(t)={t}^{4}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$  \n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=2.$ First, we substitute ${c}_{1}=0$ and  differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2{c}_{2}{t}^{3}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2{c}_{2} \\\\ 1 & ={c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={t}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c82de05a6ee9ebc9da262c7e07097557d386092961ee49ef5e1de5f6262d4318"} {"question" "q-64857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(t)=-5{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)"] ["math" "y(t)=-7{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)"] ["math" "y(t)=-{t}^{2}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))"] ["math" "y(t)={t}^{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t))"] ["math" "y(t)={t}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(t)={t}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+5y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-2,\\,x>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+5x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+5{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+5\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+5{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +5] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =-1 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-2\\pm \\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-2+\\text{i}} & =\\frac{{e}^{\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{-2-\\text{i}} & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\text{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{-2+\\text{i}}+B{x}^{-2-\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}+B\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the expression for $y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{{c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$  \n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-2.$ First, we substitute ${c}_{1}=1$ and  differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{({c}_{2}-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-(1+2{c}_{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=-2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & ={c}_{2}-2 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c82de05a6ee9ebc9da262c7e07097557d386092961ee49ef5e1de5f6262d4318"} {"question" "q-64808" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64808" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y(x)={x}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))"] ["math" "y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)"] ["math" "y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mat