stion/problem" "value" "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+20y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-4,\\,x>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+5x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+20{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+5\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+20{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +20] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-2+4\\text{i}} & =\\frac{{e}^{\\text{i}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{-2-4\\text{i}} & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\text{i}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =A{x}^{-2+4\\text{i}}+B{x}^{-2-4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =A\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}+B\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(A+B)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\text{i}(A-B)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite the expression for $y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{{c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}=A+B$ and ${c}_{2}=\\text{i}(A-B).$  \n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-4.$ First, we substitute ${c}_{1}=2$ and  differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2[(4+{c}_{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(2-2{c}_{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)]}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=-4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =-2[2-2{c}_{2}] \\\\ -4 & =-4+4{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c82de05a6ee9ebc9da262c7e07097557d386092961ee49ef5e1de5f6262d4318"} {"question" "q-64855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)=-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)"] ["math" "y(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x}"] ["math" "y(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)"] ["math" "y(x)={x}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "q-64855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6f6a17d3fd88ef5b4ee08d09fd3cfe2b030d695e7b9f7bae4f8899a10202340"} {"question" "e-6629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' + xy' + 4y = 0,\\quad x > 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/example-6629.json" "file_sha256" "21215fbdba7c5898a484b8e5bd5be251f43f9a61cd4aa2d8a0e8179d64ce4396"} {"question" "e-6629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=x^\\lambda.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +4] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=c_1 \\cos( 2 \\ln{x} )  + c_2\\sin( 2 \\ln{x} ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/example-6629.json" "file_sha256" "21215fbdba7c5898a484b8e5bd5be251f43f9a61cd4aa2d8a0e8179d64ce4396"} {"question" "e-6630" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\nt^2 y'' +5ty'+8y = 0,\\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/example-6630.json" "file_sha256" "8e51c98dfe477fc46dfcfa99423059375c8e660e17534cd5ad03e22ceea4e29d"} {"question" "e-6630" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=t^\\lambda.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda t^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)t^{\\lambda-2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+5t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+8{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+5\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+8{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +8] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+5\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t) = t^{-2} \\left( c_1 \\cos( 2 \\ln{t} ) + c_2 \\sin( 2 \\ln{t} ) \\right).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/example-6630.json" "file_sha256" "8e51c98dfe477fc46dfcfa99423059375c8e660e17534cd5ad03e22ceea4e29d"} {"question" "e-6631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' + xy' + 49 y = 0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)= c_1 \\cos(7 \\ln{x} )  + c_2\\sin(7 \\ln{x} ),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' + xy' + 49 y = 0, \\quad y(1) = 0, \\quad y'(1) = -7, \\quad x > 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/example-6631.json" "file_sha256" "ea682e7ce3c33e39d3ecdd064fbf77add56f353f0ed53203a989b274882d2292"} {"question" "e-6631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1) = 0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0 = c_1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = -7,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{7}{x}({c}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-{c}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1) = -7$ and $c_1 = 0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =7{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x) = -\\sin(7 \\ln{x} ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/example-6631.json" "file_sha256" "ea682e7ce3c33e39d3ecdd064fbf77add56f353f0ed53203a989b274882d2292"} {"question" "e-6632" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n2t^2 y'' -2ty' +20 y= 0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = t(c_1 \\cos( 3 \\ln{t} ) + c_2\\sin( 3 \\ln{t}) ),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n2t^2 y'' -2ty' +20 y= 0, \\quad y(1)= 0,  \\quad y'(1) = -1, \\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/example-6632.json" "file_sha256" "c1471a3341de6ec329d7d3c171c1c0e4f179afeeabef76f4c7f2592d85adaeb2"} {"question" "e-6632" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1) = 0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0  = c_1.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = -1,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={c}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y'(1) = -1$ and $c_1=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =3{c}_{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & ={c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t) = -\\dfrac{ t\\sin( 3 \\ln{t}) )}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070256/example-6632.json" "file_sha256" "c1471a3341de6ec329d7d3c171c1c0e4f179afeeabef76f4c7f2592d85adaeb2"}] "usage" {"q-64850" [] "e-6630" [] "e-6632" [] "q-64788" [] "e-6631" [] "e-6629" [] "q-64808" [] "q-64848" []} "basis" 2461 "retractions" [[17592186152531 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$x^{2}y^{″} + xy^{′} + y = 0, x > 0$."] [17592186152536 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$2t^{2}y^{″} - 2ty^{′} + 6y = 0, t > 0$."] [17592186152537 :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$2x^{2}y^{″} + 2xy^{′} + 32y = 0, y(1) = 1, y^{′}(1) = 0, x > 0$."] [17592186152534 :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$x^{2}y^{″} + 5xy^{′} + 5y = 0, y(1) = 1, y^{′}(1) =-2, x > 0$."] [17592186151892 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$x^2 y'' + xy' + 4y = 0,\\quad x > 0$."] [17592186151892 :question/worked-solution "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=x^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)x^{λ - 2} + x \\cdot λx^{λ - 1} + 4x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nλ(λ - 1)x^{λ} + λx^{λ} + 4x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{λ}[λ(λ - 1) + λ + 4] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ(λ - 1) + λ + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} &= -4 \\\\\nλ &= ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=c_1 \\cos(2 \\ln{x}) + c_2\\sin(2 \\ln{x})\n$$"] [17592186151893 :question/problem "Determine the general solution to the equation\n\n$t^2 y'' +5ty'+8y = 0,\\quad t\\gt 0$."] [17592186151893 :question/worked-solution "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=t^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda t^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)t^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)t^{λ - 2} + 5t \\cdot λt^{λ - 1} + 8t^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nλ(λ - 1)t^{λ} + 5λt^{λ} + 8t^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nt^{λ}[λ(λ - 1) + 5λ + 8] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ(λ - 1) + 5λ + 8 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 4λ + 8 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve the auxiliary equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 4λ + 8 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} + 4λ + 4) - 4 + 8 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 2)^{2} &= -4 \\\\\nλ + 2 &= ± 2\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= -2 ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t) = t^{-2} \\left(c_1 \\cos(2 \\ln{t}) + c_2 \\sin(2 \\ln{t}) \\right)\n$$"] [17592186151894 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$x^2 y'' + xy' + 49 y = 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y(x)= c_1 \\cos(7 \\ln{x}) + c_2\\sin(7 \\ln{x})$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$x^2 y'' + xy' + 49 y = 0, \\quad y(1) = 0, \\quad y'(1) = -7, \\quad x > 0$."] [17592186151894 :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1) = 0$ into the general solution gives\n$0 = c_1$.\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = -7$, we first differentiate $y$ to get\n$y^{′}|= \\frac{7}{x}(c_{2}\\cos (7\\ln x) - c_{1}\\sin (7\\ln x))$.\nThen, we substitute $y'(1) = -7$ and $c_1 = 0$ into the above to get\n$-7|= 7c_{2}; c_{2}|=-1$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x) = -\\sin(7 \\ln{x})\n$$"] [17592186151895 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$2t^2 y'' -2ty' +20 y= 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y = t(c_1 \\cos(3 \\ln{t}) + c_2\\sin(3 \\ln{t}))$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$2t^2 y'' -2ty' +20 y= 0, \\quad y(1)= 0, \\quad y'(1) = -1, \\quad t\\gt 0$."] [17592186151895 :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(1) = 0$ into the general solution gives\n$0 = c_1$.\n- To apply the condition $y'(1) = -1$, we first differentiate $y$ to get\n$y^{′}|= c_{2}(\\sin (3\\ln t) + 3\\cos (3\\ln t))$.\nThen, we substitute $y'(1) = -1$ and $c_1=0$ into the above to get\n$-1|= 3c_{2}; - \\frac{1}{3}|= c_{2}$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t) = -\\dfrac{t\\sin(3 \\ln{t}))}{3}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186150097 17592186150098 17592186150099 17592186150100 17592186150101 17592186150115 17592186150116 17592186150117 17592186150118 17592186150119 17592186150127 17592186150128 17592186150129 17592186150130 17592186150131 17592186150133 17592186150134 17592186150135 17592186150136 17592186150137] "immutable_fields" [[17592186150096 :answer-field/correct] [17592186150096 :answer-field/id] [17592186150096 :answer-field/key] [17592186150096 :answer-field/type] [17592186150114 :answer-field/correct] [17592186150114 :answer-field/id] [17592186150114 :answer-field/key] [17592186150114 :answer-field/type] [17592186150126 :answer-field/correct] [17592186150126 :answer-field/id] [17592186150126 :answer-field/key] [17592186150126 :answer-field/type] [17592186150132 :answer-field/correct] [17592186150132 :answer-field/id] [17592186150132 :answer-field/key] [17592186150132 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
